Chapter: RocListCalledTwice
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Ported from roc-lang/roc, src/eval/test/eval_closure_recursion_tests.zig,
 "inspect: function called multiple times with same list", case 20 in file
 order:

     { f = |lst| lst x = [1, 2] a = f(x) _b = f(x) match a { [first, ..] => first, _ => 0 } }

 and Roc expects 1.

 THE SECOND CONTROL FOR FINDING 60, and the third kind of unread binding the
 cluster supplies: case 10 discards a bare NAME (which zig refuses), case 13 a
 LITERAL, and this one a CALL RESULT. A call is the case where the discard is
 not merely allowed but REQUIRED -- the call may do work, and dropping it would
 change what runs. Together the three say exactly which values the emitter may
 stop discarding and which it must not.

 Adaptations, standing: `list-at` for the rest pattern, and `1.0` is `1`.

Section: Functions

  ident2 : List Integer -> List Integer
  ident2 (lst) = lst

  called-twice : Integer -> Integer
  called-twice (n) =
   let x = [1, 2]
   in let a = ident2 x
   in let b = ident2 x
   in list-at a 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (called-twice 0))
  end
